    - department_id
    - manager_id
Departments
    - id
    - name

join(inner join) - Returns record present in both tables. ON clause is used with  where condition
Eg. return employees had department info
 - select * from employees u join departments d on u.department_id = d.id;

left join(left outer join) - Returns record present in both tables and records present in left table. ON clause is used with  where condition
Eg. return employees along with department information
 - select * from employees u left join departments d on u.department_id = d.id;

right join(right outer join) - Returns record present in both tables and records present in right table. ON clause is used with  where condition
Eg. return all department info along with employees
 - select * from employees u right join departments d on u.department_id = d.id;

full join(full outer join) - Returns record present in both tables along with matching data in opposite table. ON clause is used with  where condition
will create uniq row for each record in both table. if row has references in other table then it will merge 2 uniq records each from both tables. It can return lots of data
Eg. return employees and department info
 - select * from employees u full join departments d on u.department_id = d.id;

cross join - map record with every other record . m*n records can be returned . ON clause is not used as it simply maps all records
Eg. return employees and department info mapping for each row
 - select * from employees u cross join departments d;
 
self join - join condition is performed on with same table (eg. employees table has manager_id. Manager is another employees). ON clause is used with where condition for column with foreign key to same table.
Eg. Return employee name , email along with manager's name, email
- select e.name, e.email, m.name, m.email from employee e join employee m on e.manager_id=m.id;